      "observed": "The CM4 pins EEPROM_nWP (pin 14) and nRPIBOOT (pin 15) are both connected to the same net NET0064. The EEPROM Write Protect switch SW1 and the Boot Select switch SW6 also both connect to NET0064.",
      "expected": "EEPROM_nWP and nRPIBOOT should be separate signals, each controlled by its own switch, as they serve different functions (EEPROM write protection and boot mode selection).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "CM4 sheet: pin 14 EEPROM_nWP and pin 15 nRPIBOOT both have wires labeled NET0064; SW1 (EEPROM Write Protect) pin 2 and SW6 (Boot Select) pin 2 both connect to NET0064."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Module1 pin 99 (Global_EN) — GLOBAL_EN net, tied directly to SW1 pin 2"}
      ],
      "impact": "The CM4 cannot independently control EEPROM write protection and boot mode selection. The two switches are shorted together, so toggling one affects the other, likely preventing proper boot configuration and EEPROM write protection.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["U32"],
      "pins": ["U32.1", "U32.3", "U32.4"],
      "nets": ["+5V", "+12V"],
      "observed": "U32 is labeled AOZ1282CI, a buck regulator, but it is wired in a boost topology (inductor from +5V to switch node, diode from switch node to +12V output) to generate +12V from +5V.",
      "expected": "A boost converter IC is required to step up +5V to +12V. A buck regulator cannot produce a higher output voltage than its input.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Power sheet: U32 AOZ1282CI with L1 10uH from +5V to SW, D40 MBR0530 from SW to +12V output."}
      ],
      "impact": "The +12V rail will not be generated correctly, so any circuits powered from +12V (e.g., the alarm connector J31) will not function.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic contains two significant anomalies: a short between the CM4's EEPROM_nWP and nRPIBOOT signals via NET0064, and a buck regulator (U32) used in a boost configuration to generate +12V from +5V, which cannot work."
}

